🔁 Method Overloading vs Overriding in Java

────────────────────────────────────────
✅ 1. Method Overloading – same name, different parameters (same class)
Also called: **compile-time / static polymorphism**

🔹 Why use it?
Perform similar tasks with different types or numbers of inputs — one clean name instead of many (`addInt`, `addDouble`, …).

```java
class Calculator {
    int add(int a, int b) {
        return a + b;
    }

    double add(double a, double b) {
        return a + b;
    }

    int add(int a, int b, int c) {
        return a + b + c;
    }
}
```

✅ Benefits:
- Code readability — one method name for similar operations
- Flexibility — different types / argument counts

────────────────────────────────────────
🔄 2. Method Overriding – same name + same parameters in a subclass
Also called: **runtime / dynamic polymorphism**

🔹 Why use it?
Provide a specific implementation of a method already defined in the parent class.

```java
class Animal {
    void sound() {
        System.out.println("Animal makes a sound");
    }
}

class Dog extends Animal {
    @Override
    void sound() {
        System.out.println("Dog barks");
    }
}

// Animal a = new Dog();
// a.sound(); // "Dog barks" — decided at runtime by object type
```

✅ Benefits:
- Runtime polymorphism — correct method chosen at runtime
- Custom behavior in subclass while keeping a common API

────────────────────────────────────────
📌 Summary

| Feature            | Overloading                         | Overriding                                      |
| ------------------ | ----------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------- |
| Also called        | Compile-time polymorphism           | Runtime polymorphism                            |
| Location           | Same class (or related)             | Parent–child (inheritance)                      |
| Parameters         | Must be different                   | Must be same                                    |
| Return type        | Can differ (with different params)  | Same or **covariant** (subtype)                 |
| Access modifier    | Can be anything                     | Cannot be more restrictive                      |
| Binding            | Compile time                        | Runtime (actual object type)                    |
| `@Override`        | Not used                            | Recommended                                     |
| `static`/`private`/`final` | Can overload                  | Cannot override (no runtime poly)               |
| Main purpose       | Flexible similar APIs               | Custom subclass behavior                        |

🎤 Quick interview traps
1) Changing **only return type** is **not** overloading.
2) Overriding needs inheritance; overloading does not.
3) `static` methods are **hidden**, not overridden.
4) `private` / `final` methods cannot be overridden.
5) Overridden method cannot throw **broader checked** exceptions than the parent.
6) Which overridden method runs depends on **object type**, not reference type.
